   "id": "t10-locker",
   "title": "1 Amazon Locker",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**What they ask:** Design a locker system where delivery drivers deposit packages and customers pick them up using a code."
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Reported:** LeetCode Discuss Mar 2025, Jun 2025 (bar raiser), Apr 2024, Apr 2026; roundz May 2025; PracHub Jul 2026. The most reported Amazon LLD question. **Full practice:** part 8, the whole mock round."
    },
    {
     "t": "table",
     "head": [
      "Bucket",
      "Ask",
      "Usual answer"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Primary capabilities",
       "Are there different sized compartments?",
       "Yes: small, medium, large"
      ],
      [
       "Primary capabilities",
       "How does the customer get the code?",
       "The system returns it; sending it is out of scope"
      ],
      [
       "Primary capabilities",
       "How does the driver get a compartment?",
       "Says the size; the system assigns one and opens it"
      ],
      [
       "Error handling",
       "Can one customer have multiple packages?",
       "Yes, one access token per package"
      ],
      [
       "Error handling",
       "Do codes expire? What happens to a package left too long?",
       "7 days; the package stays until staff remove it"
      ],
      [
       "Error handling",
       "What if all compartments of a size are full?",
       "Return an error"
      ],
      [
       "Scope boundaries",
       "Whole delivery flow, or only delivery, placement, pickup and recovery?",
       "Only placement, pickup and recovery"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Requirements**"
    },
    {
     "t": "list",
     "items": [
      "Carrier deposits a package of a specific size (S, M, L). The system assigns an available compartment; Opens it and returns an access token, or an error.",
      "Successful deposit: an access token is generated and returned. One access token per package.",
      "User retrieves a package by entering the access token. The system validates the code and opens the compartment; A specific error if the code is expired or invalid.",
      "Access tokens expire after 7 days. Expired tokens are rejected; The package stays until staff remove it.",
      "Staff can open all expired compartments, then clear each one.",
      "Out of scope: the delivery system, the notification system, how the token gets to the user."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "table",
     "head": [
      "Noun",
      "Keep?",
      "Why"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Package",
       "drop",
       "Lots of metadata we don't need; only its size, so size is a parameter of deposit"
      ],
      [
       "Compartment",
       "keep",
       "Has a size and is occupied or free"
      ],
      [
       "Locker",
       "keep",
       "Runs deposit, pickup and the expired path"
      ],
      [
       "AccessToken",
       "keep",
       "A code, when it expires, and which compartment it opens"
      ],
      [
       "Carrier / User",
       "drop",
       "External; not modelled"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "code",
     "id": "t10-locker-classes",
     "title": "Classes: − state, + behaviour",
     "code": "class Locker\n- compartments: Compartment[]  # deposit scans these for a free one of the size\n- access_tokens: Map<code, AccessToken>  (a list first is fine; a map makes pickup one step)  # pickup gets only a code; a map finds its token\n+ deposit_package(size) -> string | error  # req 1 and 2: assign, open, return the code\n+ pickup(code) -> void | error  # req 3: code to token to door\n+ open_expired_compartments() -> codes  # req 5: staff open every expired door\n+ staff_clear(code) -> void  # req 5: staff empty one and free it\n\nclass AccessToken\n- code: string  # what the customer brings; the map key\n- expiration: timestamp  # req 4: seven days after deposit\n- compartment: Compartment  # pickup must get from the code to the door\n+ is_expired() -> boolean  # req 4: one place for the expiry rule\n+ get_compartment() -> Compartment  # pickup opens this door\n+ get_code() -> string  # deposit returns it to the carrier\n\nclass Compartment\n- size: Size  (SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE -> enum)  # deposit matches on it\n- occupied: boolean  # free or taken, nothing more is needed\n+ is_occupied() -> boolean  # deposit skips taken doors\n+ mark_occupied() -> void  # deposit takes the door\n+ mark_free() -> void  # pickup and staff_clear give it back\n+ open() -> void  # req 1 and 3: the door opens for deposit and pickup\n+ get_size() -> Size  # deposit compares it to the requested size\n",
     "notes": {}
    },
    {
     "t": "say",
     "lines": [
      "**I start from the methods the requirements gave me, then ask what each one has to read.** Pickup gets only a code, so the locker keeps tokens in a map by code. The token has to reach the door, so it holds its compartment, and it holds an expiry for requirement 4. Deposit needs to find a free door of a size, so the locker holds the compartments, each with a size and an occupied flag."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "svg",
     "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 244\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Amazon Locker class map\">\n<rect class=\"d-acc\" x=\"115\" y=\"6\" width=\"190\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"d-b\" x=\"125\" y=\"26\">Locker</text><text class=\"d-s\" x=\"125\" y=\"44\">deposit, pickup, expired</text>\n<rect class=\"d-box\" x=\"20\" y=\"100\" width=\"190\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"d-b\" x=\"30\" y=\"120\">Compartment</text><text class=\"d-s\" x=\"30\" y=\"138\">size, occupied</text>\n<path class=\"d-ar\" d=\"M115 30 L10 30 L10 124 L18 124\"/>\n<rect class=\"d-box\" x=\"20\" y=\"172\" width=\"190\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"d-b\" x=\"30\" y=\"192\">AccessToken</text><text class=\"d-s\" x=\"30\" y=\"210\">code, expiration</text>\n<path class=\"d-ar\" d=\"M115 30 L10 30 L10 196 L18 196\"/>\n<rect class=\"d-dashbox\" x=\"224\" y=\"100\" width=\"180\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\"/><text class=\"d-b\" x=\"234\" y=\"120\">Size (enum)</text><text class=\"d-s\" x=\"234\" y=\"138\">SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE</text>\n<path class=\"d-ar\" d=\"M305 30 L412 30 L412 124 L406 124\"/>\n<text class=\"d-a\" x=\"14\" y=\"22\">owns</text>\n<text class=\"d-a\" x=\"352\" y=\"22\">uses</text>\n</svg>",
     "alt": "Class map"
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Core flow**"
    },
    {
     "t": "list",
     "items": [
      "deposit_package: Find a free compartment of the right size; Open it (the driver puts the package in); Mark it occupied; Generate and store an access token; Return the code.",
      "pickup: Look up the code to get its access token; Get the compartment and open it (the customer takes the package); Mark it free and remove the token.",
      "open_expired_compartments: For every expired token, open its compartment so staff can take the package; Return the codes, so staff can clear each one after emptying it.",
      "staff_clear: Staff have taken the package out: free the compartment and drop the token."
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Extensibility**"
    },
    {
     "t": "list",
     "items": [
      "Smaller package in a bigger compartment: only _get_available_compartment changes: exact size, then larger, never smaller",
      "Broken doors: occupied becomes a status with OUT_OF_SERVICE; allocation skips it; a jam at deposit tries the next compartment",
      "Did the package go in?: two-phase deposit: reserve, confirm, cancel on timeout; a RESERVED status",
      "Two drivers at once: find and mark occupied as one step: a lock, or a conditional update"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": "p",
     "text": "**Lines that set you apart**"
    },
    {
     "t": "list",
     "items": [
      "**The compartment owns what's physically inside it; the token is only a key. Deposit sets occupied, and only pickup or staff clear it.** Why: an expired code still means a package is in the box; interviewers probe exactly that.",
      "**Package isn't a class; its size is a parameter. Carrier and user are external.** Why: it shows scope judgment and keeps it to three classes in 40 minutes.",
      "**Tokens live in a map by code; codes are one-time and errors are specific (invalid, expired).** Why: pickup is one lookup, codes can't be reused, and requirement 3 asks for specific errors.",
      "**Every allocation decision goes through one helper, _get_available_compartment.** Why: size fallback, broken doors and indexing by size each change only that function."
     ]
    }
   ]
